course: religion-ethics
lesson: 56
title: The old dog, the vet and the hardest goodbye
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: When an animal is very old or ill, in pain and will not get better, a vet can give it an injection so that it dies peacefully and without pain; people call this «putting to sleep» or «putting down», but it is not real sleep, and the animal will not wake up.
explain: Picture Megan's family and their old dog, Biscuit: he can hardly stand and hurts every day, and Mum says, «We're not doing this because we don't love him. We're doing it because we do.»
explain: Many people see this as mercy, a last kindness that stops suffering; Christians often say caring for an animal includes not letting it suffer, while Buddhists and some others feel very uneasy about ending any life, and good people can feel differently.
explain: Afterwards people grieve in their own ways: Dad may cry in the car, Grandad may go quiet and busy himself on the farm, and Megan may laugh at a film a week later and then feel bad, but laughing does not mean she has forgotten.
explain: Nobody can see heaven, so nobody knows for certain where Biscuit is now; Mrs Patel at Sunday school says, «The Bible doesn't tell us clearly, but God cares for every sparrow, and I trust God with Biscuit,» which is more honest than a promise nobody can keep.
fact: «Put to sleep» and «put down» mean that a vet helps an animal to die peacefully with an injection; the animal does not wake up.
fact: Vets suggest this when an animal is very ill or in pain and cannot get better; it is often called a mercy.
fact: The animal gets very sleepy and dies within a minute or two; it is quiet, and some families choose to stay with it.
fact: Some religious people, such as many Buddhists, feel very uneasy about ending any life; people of good heart can disagree.
fact: Crying is a healthy way to let sadness out, for dads and everyone else; it is not weakness.
fact: Jesus wept when his friend Lazarus died.
fact: People grieve in different ways: some cry, some go quiet, some keep busy; no tears does not mean no love.
fact: Some animals that are very ill or old hide in a quiet place; this is instinct, not a sign that they did not love their family.
fact: Reminders like a lead or a bowl can hurt; some families put them away for a while, others keep them close, and both are fine.
fact: Vets often make a clay paw print or keep a lock of fur so families have a keepsake.
fact: Feeling happy and sad on the same day is normal; laughing does not mean you have stopped loving the one who died.
fact: The Bible does not say clearly whether animals go to heaven; Christians differ, some faiths believe in rebirth, and some people believe death is the end.
exercise: The vet says it is kindest to «put Biscuit to sleep». Will he wake up after? | No, it means he will die peacefully and not wake | Yes, after a few hours of rest | Yes, once he feels better | No, he will sleep right through until the spring comes
exercise: Megan shouts, «You're killing him!» What is the truest answer? | Biscuit is dying in pain anyway; the vet lets him die gently, as a mercy | Yes, they are killing him because they are tired of him | No, the vet is only giving him a long nap | Megan is right, so they should let him suffer
exercise: Megan asks if she can stay with Biscuit at the vet's. What is wise? | Let her choose, and someone holds her hand if she stays | Children must always wait outside | Children must always watch, whether they want to or not | She should stay but must not cry
exercise: Megan has never seen Dad cry. What is true? | Everyone cries when very sad, dads too; it is not weakness | Dads are not allowed to cry | Dad is crying because he is ill | Crying means Dad did something wrong and feels guilty about it
exercise: Biscuit's lead by the door makes Megan feel sick. What could the family do? | Put it away in a box for now and decide later | Burn it at once in the garden so nobody can see it | Make Megan look at it every day until she gets used to it | Give it to the vet to keep at the surgery for ever
exercise: Jake's old cat went missing and was found dead under a hedge. Why did she go off? | Very ill animals often hide in a quiet place by instinct | She did not like the family any more and wanted to leave them | She was hiding from Jake on purpose | She had run away to find a new home
exercise: Megan asks Mrs Patel if Biscuit is in heaven. What does Mrs Patel honestly say? | The Bible isn't clear, but she trusts God with Biscuit | Yes, every dog is certainly in heaven | No, dogs can never be with God, because they have no souls | She doesn't know what heaven is
exercise: The vet sends a clay paw print. Is it Biscuit's real paw print? | Yes, the vet pressed his paw into clay as a keepsake | No, it is copied from a picture | No, it is a toy every family gets | Yes, but only from when he was a tiny puppy, years ago
exercise: Nathan's grandad did not cry when his sheepdog was put down. Does that mean he didn't care? | No, people grieve differently, some quietly | Yes, farmers do not love their dogs the way others do | Yes, only people who cry are really sad inside | No, because the dog was not really his to love
exercise: A week after Biscuit died, Megan laughs at a film and feels terrible. Is laughing wrong? | No, you can feel happy and sad on the same day | Yes, she must stay sad for a full year after he died | Yes, laughing means she has already forgotten him | No, because Biscuit was only a dog, not a person
exercise: Who wept when his friend Lazarus died? | Jesus | Moses | Noah | David
exercise: Why do many people call putting a suffering animal down «mercy»? | It ends pain that cannot be cured | It is quicker and cheaper for the family | Animals ask for it in their own way | The law says every old dog must be put down
exercise: How do many Buddhists feel about ending an animal's life? | Very uneasy, because they try not to harm any life | Happy, because they believe it sends the animal straight to heaven | They do not care either way | They insist it must be done at once
exercise: What is the honest way for grown-ups to explain what will happen? | «Biscuit will die, very gently, and won't hurt any more» | «Biscuit is going away on a long holiday to a farm in the country» | «Biscuit is going to sleep for a while» | «Biscuit has been naughty and must go»
exercise: What happens when the vet gives the injection? | The animal gets very sleepy and dies within a minute or two | The animal jumps up and runs away | The animal sleeps for a day and then dies | The animal feels a lot of pain for an hour
test: Megan's little cousin says, «Put to sleep? Sweet, he'll wake up for tea then.» What is the kind, true reply? | No, it means Biscuit will die gently and not wake up | Yes, he'll be up by teatime | Yes, but only if we're quiet | No, he'll just wake up in the morning instead, after a long sleep
test: A Gen Z teen at school says, «Putting a dog down is literally murder, I'm so salty.» («Salty» means «bitter, angry».) What is the fairest answer? | Many see it as mercy when pain can't be cured, though some feel uneasy | Yes, vets are murderers and should go to prison | No, dogs don't feel anything, so it doesn't matter | It's fine to put a dog down whenever it's naughty
test: Megan's dad from Liverpool says, «I'm not soft, our kid, I just loved that dog.» («Our kid» means «my brother or sister», used for family.) He has been crying. What is true? | Crying shows love, not softness | Real men never cry, so he should stop | He must be ill if he is crying | He should hide in the car until he stops
test: What would happen if Biscuit's family let him go on in great pain with no hope of getting better? | He would suffer every day until he died | He would slowly get younger and stronger again | He would heal all by himself within a week | Nothing, because old dogs do not feel any pain
test: A West Country farmer says, «Proper job, old Shep's at peace now, my lover.» («Proper job» means «well done, that's right»; «my lover» is a friendly «dear».) What is he feeling? | Calm that his dog is no longer in pain | Glad the dog has run off | Angry with whoever he is talking to | In love with the person he is speaking to
test: Jake asks, «Why did our cat go away to die, did she hate us?» What is the best answer? | Ill animals hide by instinct; it was not about you | Yes, cats always run off when they stop liking their people | She was punished for being bad | She went to look for a better family
test: A Gen Alpha boy says, «Laughing when your dog died? That's so sus, no cap.» Which reply is true? | Laughing doesn't mean you've stopped loving him; it's normal | He's right, you must never laugh again | He's right, but only for a year | Laughing proves you never really loved your dog in the first place
test: A cartoon shows a vet giving a dog a needle and the dog waking up as a ghost in a bubble. What is the truthful correction? | The dog dies peacefully and does not wake up as anything | That is exactly what happens | Dogs come back as ghosts after a week | Dogs wake up as puppies after the needle
test: The vicar, speaking formally: «It is a grievous thing to lose a faithful companion, and there is no shame in tears.» What does he mean? | It is very sad to lose a loyal pet, and crying is fine | Pets are not really worth crying about, so dry your eyes | Crying in church is forbidden, even at a very sad service | People should always hide their tears from everyone else
test: Megan's mum wants to throw Biscuit's lead away at once, but Megan's brother wants to keep it. What is the wise move? | Put it in a box for now; there is no right or wrong | Throw it away; keeping old things like that is babyish | Keep it by the door so everyone sees it daily | Bury it in the garden without telling anyone
test: «My dad says our dog's definitely in heaven, my nan says no way,» says a girl. What is the most honest answer? | Nobody knows for sure; beliefs differ, and «I hope so» is honest | Dad is right because he is older | Nan is right because she goes to church | Heaven only lets in dogs that were good
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the procedure termed «putting to sleep» when performed upon a suffering animal by a veterinary surgeon? | The bringing about of a peaceful death by injection, from which the animal does not awaken | The induction of a temporary sleep from which the animal recovers | The removal of the animal to a sanctuary in the countryside | A form of punishment for undesirable behaviour
exam: Identify the principal reason for which such a procedure is ordinarily undertaken. | The relief of pain and suffering which cannot be cured | The convenience of the owners in respect of expense and of time | The animal's having reached a fixed statutory age | The owners' wish to obtain a younger animal
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the expression of grief. | Grief may be expressed by weeping, by silence or by activity, and each is genuine | Grief is genuine only where accompanied by weeping | Grief is unbecoming in adult males | Grief must cease entirely within seven days
exam: Ascertain the reason for which an aged or sickly cat may withdraw to a concealed place before death. | Instinct, and not any lack of attachment to its household | A deliberate rejection of its owners | Punishment for misconduct during its life | A desire to find a more agreeable household elsewhere in the district
